  "solutions": [
    {
      "source_subpart": "172a",
      "independent_solution": "For y >= x^2 - 4, the boundary is the upward-opening parabola with vertex (0,-4), x-axis intersections (-2,0) and (2,0). For each fixed x, take all y at least x^2-4: the region above the parabola, including its entire boundary. (0,0) and (0,-4) satisfy it; (0,-5) does not. Merely recognizing the curve is insufficient: direction and inclusion are essential."
    },
    {
      "source_subpart": "176a",
      "independent_solution": "Real squares are nonnegative. Thus x^2+y^2 >= 0 holds at every point of R^2, including (0,0), where equality holds. (1,0) also satisfies it. Removing the origin would instead describe the strict inequality; taking only the origin would describe the nonpositive inequality. The required distinction is the universal solution set, not copying its inequality."
    },
    {
      "source_subpart": "181d",
      "independent_solution": "x-y >= 3 implies x >= y+3; x+y <= 5 implies x <= 5-y. Feasibility gives y+3 <= 5-y, hence y <= 1. Combined with y >= 1, this forces y=1 and x=4. The complete intersection is the singleton {(4,1)}, with all three boundaries included. (4,1) satisfies all three. (5,1) fails x+y<=5; (3,1) fails x-y>=3; (4,2) fails both slanted bounds. A wedge, triangle of positive area, line segment of positive length, or empty set is not the intersection."
    },
    {
      "source_subpart": "182a",
      "independent_solution": "x-y=0 forces x=y=t. The other condition is t>=0. Therefore the set is {(t,t):t>=0}, the first-quadrant ray along y=x with endpoint (0,0) included. (1,1) and (0,0) satisfy it; (-1,-1) and (1,0) do not. It is neither the whole diagonal nor an open ray nor a two-dimensional half-plane."
    }
  ],
  "scope_boundary": "No other source subpart is newly reviewed. Previous independently checked mathematics, unaffected fidelity and unaffected source coverage will be reused only after both independent freezes. No images are needed for these four source subparts, whose figure_ids are empty."
